I just spent about an hour slogging through posts that were purportedly
about making NetHack harder - but they were really about making it more
interesting; and they only talked about the post-castle game anyway.

I've never gotten to the castle - I've never gotten past DLvl11 - but I
still think the game is too easy. I get disappointed when the RNG lets
me get past DLvl5 unscathed. I was fervently interested in the game for
two days or so while I learned about all the great, horrible, and
glorously arbitrary ways to die - kicking trapped doors and chests,
quaffing indiscriminately from fountains, kicking sinks, falling into
spiked pits, whacking floating eyes, starving, pissing off my god,
self-zap ID'ing wands, quaff-ID'ing potions, read-ID'ing scrolls... Now
that I've learned to avoid these things (for the most part, though I
still get silly once in a while), the game is starting to get very
boring. I liked it because of the potential to die repeatedly,
unexpectedly, and in interesting ways. I'm starting to get sick of
dying in "normal" ways like being killed by a yeti, or guards, or what
have you.

I am firmly of the opinion that the game is not stupid and arbitrary
enough. I believe it would be much improved if every step you took had
a chance to spell your utter and irreversible doom. I believe it is too
easy to find items. And I FIRMLY believe that I ought to starve more
often when my idiot wizard-self goes into a dungeon carrying no food.

I've resorted to various conducts to try to make the game harder - Zen,
foodless, eat every hallucinogen-inducing foodstuff and keep playing,
etc. (not all at the same time,of course -it's no fun if you can't have
some groundless hope of winning) but have yet to find a satisfactory
way of making it more random, arbitrary, and fun. I do still
occasionally have runs that make me feel that the RNG hasn't abandoned
me - going eight levels or so with no scrolls of identify, stepping on
a levelport trap while naked (I'd dropped all my stuff to read-ID a
scroll, as I was afraid of harming my precious magic items),  and
walking around a corner and being obliterated by a yeti was a highlight
of my day today. But all in all, I feel I'm being allowed to get away
with much too much. My suggestions are as follows:

1) Increase spell failure chances.
2) Allow wands to have B/U/C status. Cursed wands, obviously, have
horrific effects when zapped.
3) More traps. And dark rooms. And enemies that cause various kinds of
helplessness, preferably from afar.
4) Stronger enemies in general. My Level 1 elven wizards one-shot
kobolds with their quarterstaffs, when logic says that a spellcaster
going toe-to-toe with anything stronger than a lichen should get them
beaten to a bloody pulp. I say make 'em sweat: non-melee classes should
have truly sucky melee abilities, as non-spellcasting classes should
have truly abysmal spellcasting. Also, I feel like I ought to have to
run away more often. Being too badass kind of defeats the point (to me
the point is constant fear of imminent doom).
5) More corpses should do bad things when you eat them. I hear trolls
regenerate, though I've not seen one myself; what if they regenerated
*inside* of you?
6) More "oStrangulation!
7) Fountains, sinks, and thrones need to have more egregious and
humiliating consquences when you get unlucky.
8) The Oracle of Delphi should take offense when you break her statues,
and when you refuse a reading.
9) Based on comments I've read about the later game, I think steps
should be taken to make the whole lot of it even more dangerous and
arbitrary than the early levels. The game should get harder as one
goes, not easier. That would also alleviate the tedium everyone's
complaining about. Not having been there, I don't have suggestions for
this.



